---
// Page 06 — /snagging-dubai/checklist/ · DOOR · Template B
//
// The spec calls this page "the email capture", but B2 of the same spec says "No gate, no
// email wall", and 04-DESIGN forbids gating the substance. The checklist is published in
// full on the page. A PDF is offered as a convenience once the file exists — it is not the
// only way to get the content, so nothing is withheld to collect an address.
import PageShell from '../../../layouts/PageShell.astro';
import FaqList from '../../../components/FaqList.astro';
import ValuationCrossLink from '../../../components/ValuationCrossLink.astro';
import NeedsSource from '../../../components/NeedsSource.astro';
import { byPath } from '../../../lib/pages.js';
import checklist from '../../../data/snagging-checklist.json';

const page = byPath('/snagging-dubai/checklist/');

// Counted, not asserted. The title tag claims 180 points, so the page renders the real
// number and the build gate fails if the two ever stop matching.
const totalPoints = checklist.sections.reduce((n, s) => n + s.items.length, 0);

const answer =
  `A Dubai snagging checklist runs to roughly ${totalPoints} checked points across a typical ` +
  'apartment, covering mechanical, electrical and plumbing systems, joinery, waterproofing ' +
  'and glazing. The checklist below is free to download and organised room by room, so it ' +
  'can be walked in the order an inspector would actually walk the unit.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is on a Dubai snagging checklist?',
    a:
      `The list below runs to ${totalPoints} points across 20 areas, from front door alignment ` +
      'to the flood test on every bathroom floor. The categories that find the expensive ' +
      'defects are air conditioning, plumbing, waterproofing and the whole-unit thermal sweep.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I snag my own apartment with this list?',
    a:
      'You can work through most of it, and doing so before the inspector arrives is worth ' +
      'the hour. What you cannot do without instruments is thermal imaging, airflow ' +
      'measurement, water pressure testing and electrical polarity checks, which is where the ' +
      'costly defects hide.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does a snagging inspection take?',
    a:
      'A properly instrumented survey of a typical apartment is a matter of hours rather than ' +
      'minutes, because tests like the bathroom flood test and the condensate check need time ' +
      'to run. An inspection finished in twenty minutes was a walkthrough.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What should I do with the checklist once it is filled in?',
    a:
      'Issue it to the developer in writing with photographs and unit locations, keep a dated ' +
      'copy, and do not accept handover until the items are closed and re-inspected. The ' +
      'written record is what makes the defect liability claimable later.',
  },
];
---

<PageShell page={page} h1="Dubai snagging checklist" answer={answer} faqs={faqs}>
  <h2>How do I use this checklist?</h2>

  <p>
    Walk the unit in the order below, mark each point, and photograph anything that fails with
    the room and location in the frame. A defect with a photograph and a location is a defect
    the developer can act on. A defect described as “the bathroom leaks” is an argument.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do it <em>before</em> you sign for handover. Once you accept the unit, the developer’s
    one-year defect liability starts running and you are claiming rather than negotiating — see{' '}
    <a href="/snagging-dubai/developer-defect-liability/">developer defect liability</a> for
    what expires when.
  </p>

  <p>
    A printable version of this list is useful on site and is not the only way to get it, since
    the full checklist is published on this page:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="printable PDF of the checklist, generated from this page" />
  </p>

  <h2>The checklist, room by room</h2>

  <p>
    {totalPoints} points across {checklist.sections.length} areas. The last section is the one
    people skip and then need: the documents you should walk away from handover holding.
  </p>

  {
    checklist.sections.map((section) => (
      <section>
        <h3>
          {section.room} <span class="source-line">({section.items.length} points)</span>
        </h3>
        <ul>
          {section.items.map((item) => (
            <li>{item}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Which of these points actually matter most?</h2>

  <p>
    Four categories account for most of the defects that get expensive after the liability
    window closes: bathroom and balcony falls, air-conditioning performance, concealed leaks
    found by thermal imaging, and waterproofing terminations. Everything else on the list is
    worth fixing, but those are the ones where a missed defect turns into structural remedial
    work rather than a snag.
  </p>

  <p>
    What an inspection of this scope costs is set out on{' '}
    <a href="/snagging-dubai/cost/">snagging costs in Dubai</a>, and how the inspection fits
    into the wider handover sequence is on{' '}
    <a href="/handover-dubai/">the Dubai handover checklist</a>.
  </p>

  <ValuationCrossLink
    before="If the list comes back long enough that you are rethinking whether to keep the unit at all, "
    anchor="a written valuation from transactions in your building"
    after=" tells you what you would actually be walking away from."
  />

  <FaqList faqs={faqs} />
</PageShell>
